ArrayList Contract Test using Pex

In recent years, the software industry has turned to automated unit testing tools such as Pex for testing code. This tool not only provides comprehensive code coverage reports but also helps to generate a great deal of test data. 

This contract testing example demonstrates how to test an ArrayList by using Pex. It makes use of the PexClass attribute to mark the test class as a Pex test, the PexMethod attribute to indicate it's a test method and the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute to specify the state of the object under test. Inside the method, a number of property based invariants are defined for each of the public methods of the ArrayList class.

When running the tests, Pex will automatically generate a large number of test inputs that ensure the contract of the given method is maintained, as well as generate additional test inputs for other methods or properties that influence the given operations. If an assertion fails (the contract of a method is violated), Pex will use the generated test inputs to reproduce the problem. This allows it to catch and report issues that would otherwise go unreported.

```csharp
// Add method test contract
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
  // Pre-conditions 
  AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

  // Snapshot of the pre-conditions
  int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
  int Old_x = x;
  int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
  int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
  bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

  // Execute the method under test
  int New_Ret = arrList.Add(x);

  // Snapshot of the post-conditions
  int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
  int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
  int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
  bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

  // Assert that the contract is maintained after the method call 
  // (compare the change in the ArrayList state with the pre-conditions)
  PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
  PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
  PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
  PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);

  // Verify that the return value is valid
  PexAssert.IsNotNull(New_Ret);
  //... other verifications...
}
```
The above example outlines the Pex testing of an ArrayList's Add method.

