Here, the actual code generated is in C# syntax which is similar to the PexContracts you were looking for. This contract includes a series of tests for the operations you are interested in. When you run this test, it will assert that there have not been any unwanted changes in the ArrayList's state (i.e. the Count has not decreased under the test conditions). Each method is preceded by an "AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);" instruction, which helps to ensure that all other test inputs (e.g., index values, element values) are valid. Furthermore, some test instructions are outputting variables, which can significantly improve your unit tests by providing you with a detailed report showing all the variables.

This method uses the PexObserve.ValueForViewing to display values during the method execution and helps in debugging issues. NUnit is used to conduct the code compilation. Also, you could use debug points to step through your code.

I hope this information will be helpful. Again, I have to point out that the test generation using Pex is an AI tool, and while it can generate a wide range of tests, the quality of the tests can depend on your exact code base and test input.

Please let me know if you have any more questions. Enjoy coding!!!'
